Scene 1 — The Unfinished House




The passenger door stood open when Maren looked up from the camera.




She had been checking the last clip from the drive—Jonah in profile, his face lit by the dashboard, speaking too softly for the microphone—while the car idled beside the curb. She paused on a frame of his mouth forming a word she had not caught. Beyond the windshield, the new subdivision ended in raw lots and pale foundations. The nearest occupied house had one porch light on. Across the street, an unfinished two-story frame rose behind a temporary fence with one panel folded inward.




“Jonah?”




The passenger seat was empty. His jacket remained on the floor. The notebook he had kept on his lap for most of the drive was gone.




Maren stopped playback. The camera clock read 1:47 a.m. She checked the clip time. She had looked down forty-eight seconds earlier. In the frozen frame Jonah still wore his seat belt.




She leaned across the console and called through the open door. “Jonah.”




No answer came from the sidewalk, the yards, or the row of dark houses. A diesel truck moved on the highway beyond the development, low and steady for several seconds. When it passed, Maren heard plastic strike wood somewhere across the street.




She took the camera because its light was stronger than her phone’s and because she had spent six years reaching for it whenever she did not understand what was happening. The habit required no decision. Her thumb found the record switch as she stepped out.




The red tally light came on.




“Jonah, I’m recording. Say something.”




Her own voice sounded controlled in the headphones. The right channel carried the car engine and the thin scrape of wind over the microphone. The left carried her breathing.




She walked around the rear bumper. Jonah was not crouched beside the car, not behind the utility box at the lot line, not on the short concrete walk to his townhouse. His front door stood closed. He had handed Maren his keys outside Sioux City and told her to drive the rest of the way. He had not slept. For the last hour, he had repeatedly revised his account of the sound they heard at Harrow River.




A scream, at first.




Then not a scream. A long call with a break in the middle.




Then maybe an animal—except Jonah insisted he knew the difference.




Then something that had stopped when he answered it.




Maren had kept both hands on the wheel and asked him to wait until they could listen to the scouting audio. There was no scouting audio. He had left the recorder in the equipment case because they were only checking the trail and the stone shelter before the formal shoot. The camera had stayed in the car until the drive home, when Jonah asked her to turn it on.




Now the camera showed an empty street, her headlights, and the unfinished house across from them.




The temporary fence had been dragged back far enough for a person to pass. A strip of orange mesh lay twisted in the weeds. Inside the lot, tire ruts had dried into hard ridges. Maren aimed the light at the ground and found one dark print in the dust, then another. The tread matched Jonah’s boots.




She stopped at the gap.




The builder’s sign listed a number for emergencies. The black windows above the open garage reflected her light without showing anything behind them. Plastic sheeting covered part of the upper frame. Wind pressed it inward and released it. That was the sound she had heard from the car.




“Jonah, come out.”




The camera recorded the framed doorway, stacks of drywall, and a yellow extension cord disappearing into the house. She could call the police. She could call an ambulance and tell them her friend had walked into a construction site after two days without proper sleep. She could also cross the lot, find him in thirty seconds, and decide from his condition whether to call anyone.




A board shifted inside.




Maren stepped through the fence.




The garage floor had been poured, but the rest of the first level was exposed subfloor and studs. Sawdust gathered along the walls. A ladder lay on its side near the kitchen opening. She moved around it and kept the camera ahead of her, using the screen rather than the viewfinder so she could watch the floor.




“Jonah?”




A scrape sounded from deeper in the house.




She passed a room framed off beside the kitchen. The camera light flattened the studs into alternating bands of white and black. Plumbing rose through the floor near the center of the kitchen. A paper cup sat on one pipe. Nothing else moved.




The stairs began beyond the largest open room. They had treads but no railing. A smear marked the right wall stud at shoulder height. Maren moved closer.




Blood.




Not much. A partial handprint, dragged downward six inches. Fresh enough to shine.




“Jonah.”




Something struck the floor above her.




Maren lowered the camera and climbed. The unfinished stairs flexed under her weight. She kept one hand against the wall studs, then removed it when she remembered the blood. At the landing, a bare hallway ran between several rooms framed in studs. Moonlight entered through window openings covered in translucent wrap. The camera light caught nail heads, copper pipe, insulation still bound in plastic.




She heard breathing to her left.




“Jonah, it’s Maren.”




The breathing stopped.




She turned through the first doorway. Empty room. Two paint buckets. A coil of cable.




The next doorway led to a smaller room at the rear of the house. Jonah sat on the floor inside a narrow, studded frame. His knees were raised. One hand rested against his chest. The other lay palm down on a black notebook.




Maren stopped in the doorway.




The camera screen showed him more clearly than her eyes did. His hair was damp at the temples. Dirt marked one side of his face. Blood covered the knuckles of his right hand and had run into the lines of his palm. He looked at the lens first.




Then at Maren.




“Don’t,” he said.




She lowered the camera but did not stop recording. “What happened to your hand?”




“I said don’t.”




His voice changed when he saw the tally light. It became flatter, each word separated from the next. Maren had heard that voice during interviews with people who regretted inviting them into their homes. Jonah used it only when he knew the footage might outlast the moment.




She pressed the record switch.




The red light went dark.




“I stopped.”




Jonah watched the lens for another second. His shoulders dropped, not far, but enough that she saw how tightly he had been bracing them.




Maren set the camera on the floor with the lens toward the wall. “Show me your hand.”




He turned it over. Two knuckles were split. A shallow cut crossed the heel of his palm. No steady bleeding. No visible debris.




“Did you fall?”




“I don’t know.”




“You got out of the car less than five minutes ago.”




He looked past her into the open framing. “No.”




Maren waited.




“That isn’t what I mean.”




Maren crouched, leaving enough space that he did not have to move away. The room smelled of cut lumber and dust. There was no blanket, no dropped tool, no sign that anyone else had been there.




“What do you remember?”




Jonah rubbed his left thumb along the edge of the notebook. “You pulled over.”
